\import{fcap-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}

\tag{fcap}
\tag{spin}
\tag{clifford}

\taxon{Definition}
\title{The three polarized Clifford operators}
\lean/tauceti{TauCeti.SpinPolarizationData.wedge,TauCeti.SpinPolarizationData.contract,TauCeti.SpinPolarizationData.lineOperator,TauCeti.SpinPolarizationData.cliffordOperator}

\p{Let #{P} be polarization data as in \ref{fcap-001H}, over a commutative
ring #{K}, and set #{S=\bigwedge_K W}. For #{x\in W}, creation is exterior
multiplication

##{\varepsilon_x(s)=x\wedge s.}

For #{y\in W'}, let #{\varphi_y(x)=B_Q(x,y)}. Annihilation is contraction
against this functional,

##{a_y(s)=\iota_{\varphi_y}(s).}

If #{z\in L}, its operator is

##{\lambda_z=\ell(z)\,\alpha,}

where #{\alpha(s)=(-1)^r s} for homogeneous #{s\in\bigwedge^r W}. The
Clifford operator attached to the decomposed vector #{v=x+y+z} is therefore

##{c(v)=\varepsilon_x+a_y+\lambda_z.}}

\p{These symbols separate three maps that sources often denote similarly.
The canonical generator map is #{\iota_Q:V\to\Cl(Q)}; the representation
then satisfies

##{\rho(\iota_Q(x))=\varepsilon_x,\qquad
\rho(\iota_Q(y))=a_y,\qquad
\rho(\iota_Q(z))=\lambda_z.}

Thus contraction is an operator on #{S}, not another use of the symbol for
the Clifford generator. The normalization and source dictionary are fixed in
\ref{fcap-0027}.}

\p{Chevalley obtains creation and contraction from left multiplication on a
minimal ideal: one totally singular summand acts by exterior multiplication,
and the complementary summand by antiderivations
\citet{II.2.1--II.2.2, pp. 42--44}{chevalley1954algebraic}. Meinrenken's
Lagrangian-complement construction gives the same pair as
#{\varepsilon(\mu)+\iota(v)}
\citet{Section 3.2.2, Proposition 3.5, p. 57}{meinrenken2013clifford}.
Fulton--Harris independently writes the two actions as exterior multiplication
and derivation, with the derivation paired by their anticommutator form
\citet{Section 20.1, Lemma 20.9 and (20.11), pp. 304--305}{fulton2004representation}.
Fulton--Harris and Goodman--Wallach extend the model in odd dimension by
sending a normalized orthogonal generator to parity
\citet{Lemma 20.16, p. 306}{fulton2004representation}, and
\citet{Theorem 6.1.3, proof, p. 305}{goodman2009symmetry}. TauCeti packages
this last step as the supplied coordinate #{\ell:L\to K}, so that every
#{z\in L} acts by #{\ell(z)\alpha}. Chevalley's cited result is the field case
of maximal index; Meinrenken assumes a field of characteristic different from
two, a split form, and a chosen Lagrangian complement; Fulton--Harris and
Goodman--Wallach work with finite-dimensional nondegenerate complex spaces
and chosen maximal isotropics. Once #{P} is
supplied, the four displayed TauCeti operators require no additional field,
finite-dimensionality, nondegeneracy, or division-by-two hypothesis.}
